00:00:02,028 --> 00:00:03,229
the wrong account,

3
00:00:03,249 --> 00:00:05,721
and it can cost you thousands.

4
00:00:05,741 --> 00:00:07,332
Roth IRA or 401(k)?

5
00:00:07,352 --> 00:00:09,556
Everyone argues about it,

6
00:00:09,576 --> 00:00:11,396
but for most people,

7
00:00:11,416 --> 00:00:13,943
one question decides it.

8
00:00:13,963 --> 00:00:15,550
By the end of this video,

9
00:00:15,570 --> 00:00:17,053
you'll know exactly which

10
00:00:17,073 --> 00:00:18,400
account to fund first,

11
00:00:18,420 --> 00:00:19,940
and in what order.

12
00:00:19,960 --> 00:00:20,243
First,

13
00:00:20,263 --> 00:00:21,765
what are these things?

14
00:00:21,785 --> 00:00:23,423
A 401(k) is a retirement

15
00:00:23,443 --> 00:00:25,081
account you get through

16
00:00:25,101 --> 00:00:25,732
your job.

17
00:00:25,752 --> 00:00:27,449
Money comes straight out

18
00:00:27,469 --> 00:00:29,033
of your paycheck.

19
00:00:29,053 --> 00:00:31,024
An IRA is an individual

20
00:00:31,044 --> 00:00:32,466
retirement account.

21
00:00:32,486 --> 00:00:34,182
You open it yourself,

22
00:00:34,202 --> 00:00:36,504
at almost any brokerage.

23
00:00:36,524 --> 00:00:38,258
Both are just containers.

24
00:00:38,278 --> 00:00:39,104
Inside them,

25
00:00:39,124 --> 00:00:40,918
you still choose what to

26
00:00:40,938 --> 00:00:41,644
invest in,

27
00:00:41,664 --> 00:00:43,253
like index funds.

28
00:00:43,273 --> 00:00:44,762
The container decides how

29
00:00:44,782 --> 00:00:46,469
your money gets taxed.

30
00:00:46,489 --> 00:00:48,522
Here are the 2026 limits

31
00:00:48,542 --> 00:00:50,018
from the IRS.

32
00:00:50,038 --> 00:00:53,263
You can put up to $24,500

33
00:00:53,283 --> 00:00:55,285
into a 401(k).

34
00:00:55,305 --> 00:00:56,979
If you're 50 or older,

35
00:00:56,999 --> 00:00:58,738
you can add $8,000 more

36
00:00:58,758 --> 00:01:00,180
as a catch-up.

37
00:01:00,200 --> 00:01:02,542
An IRA is smaller:

38
00:01:02,562 --> 00:01:04,152
$7,500 a year,

39
00:01:04,172 --> 00:01:07,051
plus $1,100 extra at 50

40
00:01:07,071 --> 00:01:08,417
and up.

41
00:01:08,437 --> 00:01:10,345
So the 401(k) holds more.

42
00:01:10,365 --> 00:01:12,481
But bigger isn't the whole

43
00:01:12,501 --> 00:01:13,363
story.

44
00:01:13,383 --> 00:01:15,024
Here's the question that

45
00:01:15,044 --> 00:01:15,796
decides it:

46
00:01:15,816 --> 00:01:17,516
does your employer match

47
00:01:17,536 --> 00:01:18,628
your 401(k)?

48
00:01:18,648 --> 00:01:20,325
A match means your company

49
00:01:20,345 --> 00:01:22,633
adds money when you contribute.

50
00:01:22,653 --> 00:01:23,909
A common setup:

51
00:01:23,929 --> 00:01:25,893
they match 100% of what

52
00:01:25,913 --> 00:01:26,885
you put in,

53
00:01:26,905 --> 00:01:29,341
up to 4% of your salary.

54
00:01:29,361 --> 00:01:31,550
On a $60,000 salary,

55
00:01:31,570 --> 00:01:33,559
you put in $2,400,

56
00:01:33,579 --> 00:01:35,769
and they add $2,400.

57
00:01:35,789 --> 00:01:36,772
Free.

58
00:01:36,792 --> 00:01:38,726
That's an instant one hundred

59
00:01:38,746 --> 00:01:39,703
percent return,

60
00:01:39,723 --> 00:01:42,115
before you invest in anything.

61
00:01:42,135 --> 00:01:44,594
Vanguard says employer matches

62
00:01:44,614 --> 00:01:46,718
have hit a record 4.7% of

63
00:01:46,738 --> 00:01:47,072
pay.

64
00:01:47,092 --> 00:01:48,772
Check your own plan,

65
00:01:48,792 --> 00:01:51,084
because the rules vary.

66
00:01:51,104 --> 00:01:53,478
This is why the wrong first

67
00:01:53,498 --> 00:01:54,393
$1,000 hurts.

68
00:01:54,413 --> 00:01:55,943
Skip a full match,

69
00:01:55,963 --> 00:01:57,844
and you leave $1,000 on

70
00:01:57,864 --> 00:01:59,089
the table.

71
00:01:59,109 --> 00:02:01,348
Invested for 30 years at

72
00:02:01,368 --> 00:02:01,574
7%,

73
00:02:01,594 --> 00:02:03,606
which is an assumption,

74
00:02:03,626 --> 00:02:05,037
not a guarantee,

75
00:02:05,057 --> 00:02:07,145
that missing $1,000 could

76
00:02:07,165 --> 00:02:09,804
have grown to about $7,600.

77
00:02:09,824 --> 00:02:11,016
Now the tax part.

78
00:02:11,036 --> 00:02:12,922
This is the real difference

79
00:02:12,942 --> 00:02:14,481
between traditional and

80
00:02:14,501 --> 00:02:15,227
Roth.

81
00:02:15,247 --> 00:02:16,846
Traditional means a tax

82
00:02:16,866 --> 00:02:17,539
break now,

83
00:02:17,559 --> 00:02:19,500
and you pay tax later.

84
00:02:19,520 --> 00:02:21,508
Roth means you pay tax now,

85
00:02:21,528 --> 00:02:23,272
and qualified withdrawals

86
00:02:23,292 --> 00:02:25,498
in retirement are tax-free.

87
00:02:25,518 --> 00:02:27,791
Say you're in the 22% tax

88
00:02:27,811 --> 00:02:28,478
bracket,

89
00:02:28,498 --> 00:02:30,789
and you put in $1,000.

90
00:02:30,809 --> 00:02:31,761
Traditional:

91
00:02:31,781 --> 00:02:34,451
you save about $220 in federal

92
00:02:34,471 --> 00:02:36,122
tax this year.

93
00:02:36,142 --> 00:02:36,471
Roth:

94
00:02:36,491 --> 00:02:37,518
no break today,

95
00:02:37,538 --> 00:02:39,379
but every dollar of growth

96
00:02:39,399 --> 00:02:41,640
can come out tax-free later.

97
00:02:41,660 --> 00:02:43,010
So the rule of thumb:

98
00:02:43,030 --> 00:02:44,749
if you think your tax rate

99
00:02:44,769 --> 00:02:46,434
is lower now than it will

100
00:02:46,454 --> 00:02:47,488
be in retirement,

101
00:02:47,508 --> 00:02:49,047
Roth tends to win.

102
00:02:49,067 --> 00:02:50,597
That's why Roth is popular

103
00:02:50,617 --> 00:02:52,196
with people early in their

104
00:02:52,216 --> 00:02:53,046
careers.

105
00:02:53,066 --> 00:02:54,719
If you expect to earn a

106
00:02:54,739 --> 00:02:55,723
lot more later,

107
00:02:55,743 --> 00:02:56,726
paying tax now,

108
00:02:56,746 --> 00:02:58,120
at today's lower rate,

109
00:02:58,140 --> 00:02:59,691
can be a bargain.

110
00:02:59,711 --> 00:03:00,566
Quick note:

111
00:03:00,586 --> 00:03:02,651
many 401(k) plans now offer

112
00:03:02,671 --> 00:03:04,131
a Roth option too.

113
00:03:04,151 --> 00:03:06,149
The match works the same

114
00:03:06,169 --> 00:03:06,885
way.

115
00:03:06,905 --> 00:03:09,021
So why do people love the

116
00:03:09,041 --> 00:03:11,090
Roth IRA specifically?

117
00:03:11,110 --> 00:03:11,378
One:

118
00:03:11,398 --> 00:03:13,102
you can usually take out

119
00:03:13,122 --> 00:03:14,711
what you contributed at

120
00:03:14,731 --> 00:03:15,343
any time,

121
00:03:15,363 --> 00:03:16,895
without tax or penalty.

122
00:03:16,915 --> 00:03:18,789
Just not the earnings.

123
00:03:18,809 --> 00:03:20,247
That makes it a flexible

124
00:03:20,267 --> 00:03:21,424
first account.

125
00:03:21,444 --> 00:03:21,699
Two:

126
00:03:21,719 --> 00:03:23,458
you pick the brokerage and

127
00:03:23,478 --> 00:03:24,118
the funds,

128
00:03:24,138 --> 00:03:25,823
so you can choose low-cost

129
00:03:25,843 --> 00:03:26,992
index funds.

130
00:03:27,012 --> 00:03:27,345
Three:

131
00:03:27,365 --> 00:03:29,157
there are income limits.

132
00:03:29,177 --> 00:03:30,190
For 2026,

133
00:03:30,210 --> 00:03:33,099
your ability to contribute

134
00:03:33,119 --> 00:03:35,915
phases out between $153,000

135
00:03:35,935 --> 00:03:38,731
and $168,000 of income for

136
00:03:38,751 --> 00:03:40,632
single filers.

137
00:03:40,652 --> 00:03:43,116
And between $242,000 and

138
00:03:43,136 --> 00:03:45,967
$252,000 for married couples

139
00:03:45,987 --> 00:03:47,931
filing jointly.

140
00:03:47,951 --> 00:03:49,344
So here's the order that

141
00:03:49,364 --> 00:03:51,010
works for most people.

142
00:03:51,030 --> 00:03:51,646
Step one:

143
00:03:51,666 --> 00:03:53,322
put enough in your 401(k)

144
00:03:53,342 --> 00:03:54,883
to get the full match.

145
00:03:54,903 --> 00:03:56,786
Never skip free money.

146
00:03:56,806 --> 00:03:57,828
Step two:

147
00:03:57,848 --> 00:04:00,765
open a Roth IRA and fund

148
00:04:00,785 --> 00:04:01,144
it,

149
00:04:01,164 --> 00:04:03,608
if your income allows.

150
00:04:03,628 --> 00:04:05,334
Up to $7,500.

151
00:04:05,354 --> 00:04:06,437
Step three:

152
00:04:06,457 --> 00:04:09,066
go back to your 401(k) and

153
00:04:09,086 --> 00:04:09,999
add more,

154
00:04:10,019 --> 00:04:12,688
toward the $24,500 limit.

155
00:04:12,708 --> 00:04:13,927
No match at work?

156
00:04:13,947 --> 00:04:15,637
Then you can start with

157
00:04:15,657 --> 00:04:17,866
the Roth IRA as step one.

158
00:04:17,886 --> 00:04:19,690
And if you have high-interest

159
00:04:19,710 --> 00:04:20,762
credit card debt,

160
00:04:20,782 --> 00:04:22,318
paying that down usually

161
00:04:22,338 --> 00:04:24,327
comes before all of this.

162
00:04:24,347 --> 00:04:24,598
So,

163
00:04:24,618 --> 00:04:26,959
back to your first $1,000.

164
00:04:26,979 --> 00:04:28,855
If your job has a match,

165
00:04:28,875 --> 00:04:30,561
it goes into the 401(k),

166
00:04:30,581 --> 00:04:32,709
until you get every matching

167
00:04:32,729 --> 00:04:33,614
dollar.

168
00:04:33,634 --> 00:04:34,654
If it doesn't,

169
00:04:34,674 --> 00:04:36,927
a Roth IRA is a great place

170
00:04:36,947 --> 00:04:38,042
to start.

171
00:04:38,062 --> 00:04:39,417
That's the one question:

172
00:04:39,437 --> 00:04:41,098
is there free money on the

173
00:04:41,118 --> 00:04:41,854
table?

174
00:04:41,874 --> 00:04:43,430
Three common mistakes to

175
00:04:43,450 --> 00:04:44,211
avoid.

176
00:04:44,231 --> 00:04:44,980
Mistake one:

177
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:46,685
contributing less than the

178
00:04:46,705 --> 00:04:48,335
match because money feels

179
00:04:48,355 --> 00:04:48,720
tight.

180
00:04:48,740 --> 00:04:50,315
That's the most expensive

181
00:04:50,335 --> 00:04:52,035
dollar you can skip.

182
00:04:52,055 --> 00:04:52,868
Mistake two:

183
00:04:52,888 --> 00:04:54,594
putting money in a Roth

184
00:04:54,614 --> 00:04:56,260
IRA and never investing

185
00:04:56,280 --> 00:04:56,498
it.

186
00:04:56,518 --> 00:04:58,045
It just sits in cash.

187
00:04:58,065 --> 00:05:00,231
You have to pick a fund.

188
00:05:00,251 --> 00:05:01,371
Mistake three:

189
00:05:01,391 --> 00:05:03,224
cashing out your 401(k)

190
00:05:03,244 --> 00:05:05,006
when you change jobs.

191
00:05:05,026 --> 00:05:07,073
You can usually roll it

192
00:05:07,093 --> 00:05:09,212
into an IRA or your new

193
00:05:09,232 --> 00:05:10,681
plan instead.

194
00:05:10,701 --> 00:05:12,671
Also check your plan's vesting

195
00:05:12,691 --> 00:05:13,240
schedule.

196
00:05:13,260 --> 00:05:14,888
Some matches only become

197
00:05:14,908 --> 00:05:16,537
fully yours after a few

198
00:05:16,557 --> 00:05:18,359
years at the company.

199
00:05:18,379 --> 00:05:19,643
Quick recap.

200
00:05:19,663 --> 00:05:20,590
401(k) first,

201
00:05:20,610 --> 00:05:22,885
up to the full match.

202
00:05:22,905 --> 00:05:24,086
Then a Roth IRA,

203
00:05:24,106 --> 00:05:25,947
if you're under the income

204
00:05:25,967 --> 00:05:26,828
limits.

205
00:05:26,848 --> 00:05:29,151
Then back to the 401(k).

206
00:05:29,171 --> 00:05:30,979
Roth means pay tax now.

207
00:05:30,999 --> 00:05:32,937
Traditional means pay tax

208
00:05:32,957 --> 00:05:33,794
later.

